<Layout title="About">
  <h1>About</h1>
  <p>
    Hi, I'm Morgan! I'm finishing my PhD in the joint UC Berkeley/UCSF
    Bioengineering program, where I've gotten to do what I love most, which
    is working on incredible science while staying connected to my
    community. In Dr. Kevin Healy's lab, I engineered hyaluronic acid
    hydrogels that help the body regenerate tissue after complex soft tissue
    injuries, like rotator cuff tears. My interdisciplinary background in
    hydrogel engineering, biochemistry, and cell-material interactions
    prepared me for the complexity of this challenge, which is designing a
    therapeutic that is effective, safe, and usable by <em>anyone</em>,
    regardless of their biology or geography. As a co-inventor of part of the
    technology behind MuscleMatrix, and now a scientific consultant helping
    build the company, I get to see this work move from the bench toward
    the clinic, where it could help patients directly.
  </p>

  <p>
    My love of science started back in middle school, and what has grown
    since then is my sense of who science is for. Living in Boston and
    Oakland, being a queer, Jewish woman in STEM, and meeting people from so
    many different backgrounds have shown me how amazing and how essential
    diversity is to successful science. These experiences are what draw me
    toward advocacy, outreach, and policy within STEM, so that the people
    doing science, and the people it serves, reflect all of us.
  </p>

  <p>
    Before Berkeley, I got to explore a huge range of science, and each stop
    shaped how I approach research today. In the Directed Assembly of
    Particles and Suspensions lab, I fabricated dual-network hydrogels with
    magnetically responsive fiber reinforcement for bone grafts, ran COMSOL
    simulations to explore new toughening mechanisms, and built freeze-cast
    nerve repair conduits with collaborators at Dartmouth College and
    Harvard Medical School. That work taught me how much materials science
    and mechanics matter for a device to survive inside the body. I also
    screened CRISPR guide RNAs at Editas Medicine, searched for druggable
    targets for cancer and sickle cell disease at UConn Health and Vertex
    Pharmaceuticals, and worked hands-on with patients as an EMT and
    Clinical Care Technician. That last experience matters most to me,
    because it's the reason I never think about a hydrogel as purely a
    materials problem. Somewhere at the other end is a patient, and that's
    who I'm really designing for.
  </p>

  <p>
    That same belief carries into how I spend my time outside the lab. I
    volunteer weekly with Food Not Bombs East Bay, and I care deeply about
    mutual aid and the social and political forces that determine who
    actually gets access to science, medicine, and care. I'm also passionate
    about <a href="/teaching/">teaching</a> and about helping other people
    find and chase their own passions in STEM, whether that's a fellow
    researcher or someone who has never thought of themselves as "a science
    person." If that's you, I'd love to talk!
  </p>

